SigmaSelmer.of_upstream identifies the module with SelmerIwasawaCohomology:L2/galois-selmer-group.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L0/primitive-imprimitive-comparison
Let T, (T_v) and A be as in L0/sigma-primitive-selmer with A noetherian normal, F any number field,
and S a finite set of finite places not above p at which T is unramified, contained in Σ. Then (i)
Ch^Σ_{F,A}(T) ⊇ Ch^{Σ∖S}_{F,A}(T) · ∏_{v∈S} det_A(1 − q_v^{-1} frob_v^{-1} | T) (SU Lemma 3.2.15,
Cor. 3.2.16); (ii) over ℚ_∞, the Pontryagin dual of H¹_Σ(ℚ_∞, T ⊗ A^∨) = ∏_{v∈Σ, v∤p} H¹(ℚ_{∞,v}, T
⊗ A^∨) is a finitely generated torsion Λ_{ℚ,A}-module with no nonzero pseudo-null submodule, and its
characteristic ideal is generated by ∏_{ℓ∈Σ} P_{T,ℓ}(ℓ^{-1} ε_ℚ(frob_ℓ)), P_{T,ℓ}(X) = det(1 − X
frob_ℓ^{-1} | (T⊗_A Frac A)^{I_ℓ}) (SU Lemma 3.2.17); (iii) when the primitive dual is torsion and
the global-to-local map is surjective (Greenberg–Vatsal), Ch^Σ = Ch^∅ · ∏_{ℓ∈Σ}
P_{T,ℓ}(ℓ^{-1}ε(frob_ℓ)) (SU Prop. 3.2.18). On the analytic side the Σ-imprimitive p-adic
L-functions are defined by multiplying the primitive ones by the same Euler-factor elements, so
every removed factor is matched by the identical element of Λ.
Hypothesis: For (i), A is noetherian normal and T is unramified at S, a finite set away from p. For
the local cyclotomic module in (ii), use the compact finite-type coefficients of SU §3.2.2; the
characteristic-divisor assertion also requires normal noetherian A.
Hypothesis: For the exact sequence in (iii), A admits decreasing ideals I_n with intersection zero
and each A/I_n finite free over Z_p; the primitive X_Q∞(T,T_p) is Λ_Q,A-torsion; T* (the Pontryagin
dual in SU §3.1.3) has no nonzero A-submodule with trivial G_Q action; Σ∪{p} contains ram(T). For
every finite Σ′⊂Σ, localization onto H¹_{Σ\Σ′} is then surjective (SU Prop. 3.2.18).
Hypothesis: The Euler polynomial of Lemma 3.2.17 uses inverse frob_ℓ on rational inertia invariants.
A rank-one invariant space gives 1−αX, where α is the eigenvalue of that precise inverse Frobenius.
Integral lattices and analytic conventions must be compared explicitly.
Check: Transfer a primitive/imprimitive bound only after matching identical Euler elements on the
arithmetic and analytic sides; do not cancel a vanishing specialization.
Check: A Steinberg Euler element is a unit in the local power-series ring exactly when its residue
at augmentation is nonzero. With ℓ=11, p=5 and α=1, 1−ℓ^{-1}γ has augmentation 10/11, so it is not a
unit even though the residual representation can be ramified.
Source: SU-2014, Lemma 3.2.15 and Cor. 3.2.16, author-copy p. 26; Lemma 3.2.17, pp. 26–27; Prop.
3.2.18, p. 27.
Source: CLW-v1, proof of Thm 8.2.1, p. 79.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L0/selmer-specialization-control
Let F_∞ be ℚ_∞, K_∞ or K^+_∞, A a profinite ℤ_p-algebra, 𝔞 ⊂ A an ideal and T as in
L0/sigma-primitive-selmer. Assume the explicit SU Prop. 3.2.8 hypotheses below, so that
X^Σ_{F_∞}(T)/𝔞 ≅ X^Σ_{F_∞}(T/𝔞T). Then (i) Ft^Σ_{F,A/𝔞}(T/𝔞T) = Ft^Σ_{F,A}(T) mod 𝔞; (ii) if A and
A/𝔞 are noetherian normal domains and (f) ⊇ Ch^Σ_{F,A}(T) is principal, then f mod 𝔞 divides
Ch^Σ_{F,A/𝔞}(T/𝔞T); in particular when A is factorial Ch^Σ_{F,A}(T) mod 𝔞 divides
Ch^Σ_{F,A/𝔞}(T/𝔞T). (iii) For the descent K_∞ → K^+_∞ along I^- = ker(Λ_K → Λ_ℚ), X^Σ_{K_∞}(T)/I^- ≅
X^Σ_{K^+_∞}(T), and when A is factorial Ch^Σ_{K_∞}(T) mod I^- divides Ch^Σ_{K^+_∞}(T).
Hypothesis: In SU Prop. 3.2.8, inertia at p acts on T/T_p through Γ_Q; Σ∪{p} contains ram(T); F∞ is
Q∞, K∞ or K⁺∞; T* (the Pontryagin dual in SU §3.1.3) has no nonzero A-subquotient on which G_F acts
trivially. These hypotheses apply for every ideal 𝔞 under consideration, not merely a presumed
control isomorphism.
Hypothesis: The control isomorphism for a general ideal 𝔞 of the coefficient ring is SU Prop. 3.2.8
and is owned here; the upstream derived control (SelmerIwasawaCohomology:L3) covers the
specialization to finite layers of a one-variable tower and is used only there. The control
isomorphism needs SU Prop. 3.2.8's hypotheses; without them only a map with kernel and cokernel
controlled by H⁰ and local terms exists.
Hypothesis: Characteristic ideals do not commute with base change in general; only the principal
majorant transfers (ii). A specialization at which f vanishes gives no information.
Check: Vanishing check: if f ∈ 𝔞 the conclusion of (ii) is empty, and no cancellation of f against
the specialized characteristic ideal is allowed.
Check: Used by L1/su-cyclotomic-divisibility (descent from two variables to the cyclotomic line) and
by L5a/quartic-cm-selmer-restriction.
Source: SU-2014, Prop. 3.2.8, p. 23; Cor. 3.2.9, Prop. 3.2.11, p. 24.
Source: BCS-v2, proof of Lemma 5.1.1, p. 9.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L0/eisenstein-ideal
Let Λ be a noetherian complete local domain, 𝕋 a reduced finite Λ-algebra generated over Λ by the
images of a set 𝒯 of Hecke operators acting on a space of Λ-adic cusp forms, and λ: 𝒯 → Λ the system
of eigenvalues of a Λ-adic Eisenstein family E (a Λ-algebra homomorphism from the abstract Hecke
algebra). The Eisenstein ideal I_E ⊆ 𝕋 is the ideal generated by t − λ(t) for t ∈ 𝒯; when the
structure map Λ → 𝕋/I_E is surjective, the Eisenstein ideal of Λ is its kernel E_E, so that Λ/E_E ≅
𝕋/I_E. When I_E is proper, 𝕋_E denotes the localization of 𝕋 at the maximal ideal containing I_E.
Hypothesis: 𝕋 is generated over Λ by 𝒯 together with the U_p-operators, so Λ → 𝕋/I_E is surjective;
otherwise E_E is defined as the kernel on the image.
Hypothesis: The Hecke operators are those at primes outside a fixed finite set Σ, the U_p-operators,
and the central or diamond operators used by the source.
Check: The congruence statements of L1, L2 and L2s are lower bounds ord_P(E_E) ≥ ord_P(L) for
height-one primes P of Λ, from a cuspidal family congruent to E.
Source: SU-2014, §6.5.3, pp. 97–98, (6.5.3.a).
Source: FW-v3, Def. 7.30, p. 96.
Source: CLW-v1, proof of Thm 8.2.1, p. 79.
API EisensteinIdeal.hecke: I_E ⊆ 𝕋 generated by t − λ(t), t ∈ 𝒯.
API EisensteinIdeal.base: E_E := ker(Λ → 𝕋/I_E).
API EisensteinIdeal.quotientEquiv: Λ/E_E ≃ 𝕋/I_E as Λ-algebras when the structure map is onto.
API EisensteinIdeal.localize: Formation of I_E and E_E commutes with localization at a height-one
prime P of Λ and completion.
API EisensteinIdeal.proper_iff: For a unital structure map, E_E≠⊤ iff I_E≠⊤. For finite local
Λ-algebras, this is equivalent to existence of a residue-field cuspidal eigensystem satisfying the
specified congruences.
API EisensteinIdeal.mono: Enlarging 𝒯 enlarges I_E; restricting to a direct factor of 𝕋 enlarges
E_E.
API EisensteinIdeal.ext: Equality of the generated Hecke ideals implies equality of their
contractions; membership in E is equivalent to the image in 𝕋/I being zero.
Test eisensteinIdeal_ramanujan_level1: For weight 12, level 1 cusp forms over ℤ_691 with E = E_12
(λ(T_ℓ) = 1 + ℓ^{11}), 𝕋 = ℤ_691 and E_E = (691), since τ(ℓ) ≡ 1 + ℓ^{11} mod 691 and the numerator
of B_12/24 has 691-adic valuation one.
Test eisensteinIdeal_no_congruence: If no cusp form is congruent to E, then I_E = 𝕋 and E_E = Λ (for
example weight 12 at p = 5).
Test eisensteinIdeal_mazur: In a weight-two prime-level Mazur setting, I_E in the cuspidal Hecke
algebra is generated by the appropriate T_ℓ−1−ℓ and 1+w_N. E_E is its contraction along Λ→𝕋, an
ideal in Λ; it is not identified with that Hecke ideal.
Test eisensteinIdeal_not_congruence_ideal: E_E differs from the congruence ideal of a cuspidal
eigenform: for weight 12, level 1 and p = 691 the congruence ideal of Δ in the cuspidal Hecke
algebra is the unit ideal (𝕋 = ℤ_p), while E_E = (691).

### AutomorphicCongruences:L0/su-lattice-datum
Let H = G ⋊ {1, c} with c of order two, A a complete discrete valuation ring with fraction field F
and maximal ideal m, and R a local reduced finite flat A-algebra with R/M = A/m. A lattice datum
consists of: (1) a character ν: H → A^×; (2) a character χ: G → A^× with χ̄ ≠ ν̄χ̄^{-c}, and χ′ :=
νχ^{-c}; (3) a representation ρ: G → Aut_A(V), V ≅ A^n, with ρ^c ≅ ρ^∨ ⊗ ν, ρ̄ absolutely
irreducible and ρ residually disjoint from χ and χ′ (some x ∈ A[G] has coprime residual
characteristic polynomials); (4) a representation σ: G → Aut_{R⊗F}(M), M ≅ (R ⊗ F)^{n+2}, with σ^c ≅
σ^∨ ⊗ ν, tr σ(G) ⊆ R and finitely generated R[G]-orbits; (5) a proper ideal I ⊂ R with J := A ∩ I ≠
0, A/J ≅ R/I and tr σ ≡ χ′ + tr ρ + χ mod I. Local data at a subgroup D ⊂ G: a D-stable free summand
M^+ ⊆ M with free quotient, a D-stable free summand V^+ ⊆ V, and the congruence Ch(M^+, x, T) ≡
Ch(V^+, x, T)(1 − Tχ(x)) mod I for x ∈ R[D]. The datum is ν-polarized when ν is compatible (Φ^t =
−ν(c)Φ) with the polarization of ρ.
Hypothesis: The polarization conditions are those of SU 4.2.2; residual disjointness is in the sense
of SU 4.1.2.
Hypothesis: In applications G = G_{K,Σ}, H = G_{ℚ,Σ}, D a decomposition group at a prime above p, A
the completion of a localization of a two-variable Iwasawa algebra at a height-one prime P, and R a
completed local Hecke algebra.
Hypothesis: SU §4.3 requires the common residue field κ to have at least n+2 nonzero elements. To
deduce compatibility of ν with σ from Lemma 4.3.3, require char κ≠2 and absolute irreducibility of
every field factor σ_i; otherwise compatibility with σ is a separate hypothesis of the Selmer
consequence.
Hypothesis: SU Ch(θ,x,T)=det(1−Tθ(x)); convert Mathlib’s det(TI−θ(x)) before invoking polynomial
congruences.
Check: The SU instance (SU 7.3.2), the CLW instance (proof of CLW Thm 8.2.1, items (1)–(5)) and the
FW instance (proof of FW Thm 7.32) are all lattice data with n = 2.
Source: SU-2014, §4.1.2, Lemma 4.1.3, p. 48; §4.2.1–4.2.3, pp. 48–49; §4.5.1–4.5.3, p. 59.
Source: CLW-v1, proof of Thm 8.2.1, p. 79.
Source: WAN-RS, introduction, p. 4.
API LatticeDatum.chiPrime: χ′ = νχ^{-c}.
API LatticeDatum.trace_congr: tr σ ≡ χ′ + tr ρ + χ modulo I.
API LatticeDatum.residualProjectors: The elements x_1, x_2 ∈ R[G] separating the residually disjoint
constituents (SU Lemma 4.1.3).
API LatticeDatum.localize: Given descent data satisfying SU §4.4 and the freeness/residual
conditions, localization and completion produce the datum; preservation of absolute irreducibility
and disjointness must be checked, not inferred for arbitrary base change.
API LatticeDatum.localData: The D-stable summands M^±, V^± with the congruence of characteristic
polynomials and its consequence for the quotients.
API LatticeDatum.toGMA: The R[G]-span of a lattice in M carries a generalized-matrix-algebra
structure of type (1, n, 1) relative to the residual decomposition.
API LatticeDatum.mk: Construct from the five SU §4.2.3 fields, compatibility with ρ, and the
explicitly specified local data.
API LatticeDatum.ext: Isomorphisms of the coefficient representations preserving χ,ν,I and the
chosen local summands transport the datum and its trace/polynomial identities.
Test latticeDatum_ribet_case: Rank n=0 is excluded by the absolutely irreducible middle-constituent
requirement. A bare two-character Ribet datum cannot be passed to this constructor; the rank-two
Ribet theorem is a distinct supplier contract.
Test latticeDatum_su_instance: In SU 7.3.2, χ = σ_{χ̄_f,0}σ^c_ψ ε^{-3}, ρ_0 = ρ_f ⊗
σ_{χ̄_f,0}σ^{-c}_ξ σ^c_ψ ε^{-2}, χ′ = σ_{χ̄_f,0}ε^{-1}det ρ_f σ^{-1}_{ξ′}σ^c_ψ, and ν is fixed by
χ^cχ′ = σ^2_{χ̄_f,0}ε^{-7}ε_ℚ^{-2}; these satisfy χ′ = νχ^{-c}, and the trace of ρ_D in (6.5.2.a) is
χ + tr ρ_0 + χ′.
Test latticeDatum_requires_distinct: If χ̄ = ν̄χ̄^{-c} then condition (2) fails; SU (dist) and CLW's
ξ_p ≢ ξ_p̄ are exactly what rule this out.
Test latticeDatum_gma_agreement: For a lattice datum with σ residually multiplicity free,
LatticeDatum.toGMA agrees with IntegralHeckeAndGaloisDeterminants:IHG.1/generalized-matrix-algebra.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L0/lattice-construction
Let (ν, χ, ρ, σ, I) be a ν-polarized lattice datum (L0/su-lattice-datum) with H = G_{ℚ,Σ}, G =
G_{K,Σ}, local data at D = G_{K,p} satisfying SU 4.5.1–4.5.3, with V^+_0 ⊕ A(χ) and V^-_0 ⊕ A(χ′)
residually disjoint modulo P, where A = Â_{0,P} for a height-one prime P of a noetherian normal
domain A_0, and J = A ∩ I. Then (i) ord_P Ch^Σ_ℚ(ψν) ≥ ℓ_P(L(χ′)/J(χ′)); (ii) ord_P Ch^Σ_K(ρ_0 ⊗
χ^{-1}) ≥ ℓ_P(N/N′), and in particular ord_P Ch^Σ_K(ρ_0 ⊗ χ^{-1}) ≥ ord_P(J) when N′ = 0 (SU Prop.
4.5.6); (iii) if ord_P Ch^Σ_ℚ(ψν) = 0 then ord_P Ch^Σ_K(ρ_0 ⊗ χ^{-1}) ≥ ord_P(J) (SU Cor. 4.5.7);
(iv) under SU Lemma 4.3.11, if ord_P(J) > ord_P Ch^Σ_ℚ(ψν) then ord_P Ch^Σ_K(ρ_0 ⊗ χ^{-1}) ≥ 1 (SU
Prop. 4.5.8). Here the Selmer groups are the Σ-primitive ones of L0/sigma-primitive-selmer for the
pairs (A_0(ψν), 0) and (V_0(χ^{-1}), V^+_0(χ^{-1})). The variant of Wan20 Prop. 9.2 replaces the
irreducibility of σ by condition (9): every specialization σ_λ is absolutely irreducible or contains
an absolutely irreducible two-dimensional subrepresentation with trace ≡ χ + χ′ modulo I.
Hypothesis: P is a height-one prime of A_0 with A_0 noetherian normal; the polarization ν is
compatible with the polarization of ρ (4.5.5.a).
Hypothesis: The bound concerns the Σ-primitive groups; primitive versions follow by
L0/primitive-imprimitive-comparison.
Hypothesis: The excluded case ord_P Ch^Σ_ℚ(ψν) > 0 is where the abelian Selmer group may absorb the
extensions; CLW and Wan exclude such P explicitly.
Hypothesis: Use SU §4.4 descent: A_0 is a profinite Krull domain with height-one P and completion A;
R_0 is a reduced local finite A_0-algebra with Q over P and completed R; ρ descends to the free
A_0-representation V_0, σ has trace in R_0 and finite R_0[G]-orbits, and I_0,J_0 are the
contractions. G,H are profinite and representations continuous.
Hypothesis: For SU §4.5.5 require ν compatible with the polarization Φ of σ, and V⁺_0 an A_0-free
direct summand stable under G_Qp. This compatibility follows from Lemma 4.3.3 only in odd residue
characteristic with each σ_i absolutely irreducible.
Hypothesis: The Wan20 Prop. 9.2 variant requires all its setup (1)–(9), including a rank-one local
M⁺ character congruence and disjoint M⁻ constituent. Its condition (9) permits a two-dimensional
absolutely irreducible subrepresentation whose trace is congruent to χ+χ′, not χ+χ^{-c} unless ν=1.
The auxiliary one-dimensional Selmer factor must have valuation zero for the full length-of-J bound.
Check: A height-one prime with ord_P(J) = 2 and N′ = 0 must give ord_P(Ch) ≥ 2, not just ≥ 1.
Check: Excluded-prime check: when ord_P Ch^Σ_ℚ(ψν) > 0 and Lemma 4.3.11 fails, no conclusion is
drawn.
Check: The extension classes are those of the automorphic family; the theorem does not presuppose
any divisibility of characteristic ideals.
Source: SU-2014, §4.3–4.5, Props 4.4.4–4.4.5, 4.5.4, 4.5.6, Cor. 4.5.7, Prop. 4.5.8, pp. 52–61.
Source: CLW-v1, proof of Thm 8.2.1, pp. 79–80.
Source: FW-v3, proof of Thm 7.32, pp. 96–97.
Source: WAN-RS, §9, setup (1)–(9) and Prop. 9.2, PDF pp. 93–94.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L0/extension-class-local-conditions
In the situation of L0/lattice-construction, the classes [c_φ] obtained from the off-diagonal blocks
of σ satisfy, after multiplication by an element t ∉ P: (a) at every place v ∉ Σ they are
unramified, since σ is unramified outside Σ; (b) at the primes above p they lie in the kernel of
restriction to H¹(D, V^-_0 ⊗ A_0^∨(χ^{-1})) for D = G_{K,p} (the ordinary condition), and by the
polarization the corresponding statement holds at G_{K,p̄}; (c) at places v ∈ Σ, v ∤ p, no condition
is imposed (Σ-primitive). At a Steinberg place, pass between Σ-primitive and primitive objects using
the precise Euler element of primitive-imprimitive-comparison; residual ramification alone does not
make that element a unit.
Hypothesis: The residual disjointness of V^+ ⊕ A(χ) and V^- ⊕ A(χ′) modulo P (SU (7.3.3.a)) is what
produces the D-projector; it is verified in each instance (SU proof of Thm 7.3.5, CLW and FW
checks).
Check: Checks the three kinds of local condition named by the stage: unramified, Steinberg via
relaxing and Euler factors, ordinary at p.
Source: SU-2014, Prop. 4.5.4, pp. 59–60; proof of Thm 7.3.5, p. 107.
Source: FW-v3, Thm 1.6(2), p. 6; Assumption 3.7, p. 25.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L0/canonical-period-congruence-number
For the p-stabilized ordinary newform f over a finite integral Z_p-algebra R satisfying (irred) and
(dist), SU Lemma 12.2.2 gives η_f = u_f·2^{-3}(2i)^{κ+1} S(f)·⟨f,f^c|W_{p^rM}⟩/(Ω_f^+Ω_f^-), with
u_f∈R^× and S(f) the explicit stabilization correction of SU (11.7.3.b). Under the stated
integral/Gorenstein hypotheses η_f generates the congruence ideal. For a primitive ordinary family
over an integral finite Λ-algebra I satisfying (irred),(dist), the family congruence generator η_I
specializes to the η_f choices (SU pp. 194–195); Lemma 12.2.7 supplies the integral functional used
in the canonical-period-normalized Eisenstein coefficients. No canonical/Gross period comparison or
unproved identification of EPW homology and SU cohomology period lattices is used.
Hypothesis: (irred) and (dist) for f, which give the freeness of the relevant Hecke modules and the
Gorenstein property used to make the congruence module cyclic.
Hypothesis: The unit u_{f_φ} in SU Thm 12.3.1(ii) depends only on f_φ and is not specified further.
Check: The integral normalization is the one in which the lattice construction measures congruences;
replacing Ω^+Ω^- by a non-integral period breaks the integrality of ℰ_D's coefficients.
Source: SU-2014, Thm 12.3.1 (i)–(ii), p. 199; Thm 12.4.1, pp. 202–203.
