Test gu22Forms_eisenstein_not_cuspidal: For the Eisenstein family with nonzero boundary value, ℰ_D
belongs to M^1 and not M^0. This rejects confusing the boundary kernel with the full space or the
forms with their compact duals.
Test gu22Forms_projector_idempotent: e ∘ e = e and e commutes with every T_v; on a classical form of
weight a whose u_t-eigenvalue is a p-adic unit, e acts as the identity.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L1/gu22-hida-control
For n = 2 and q = 0 (cusp forms) or q = 1 (all forms): (i) V^q_{a,ord} is free of finite rank over
Λ_n, and its specializations at finite-order characters ψ of level s have rank independent of ψ and
of the weight (SU Thm 6.3.3); (ii) for any Λ_n-algebra A there is a short exact sequence 0 →
M^0_{a,ord}(K^p, A) → M^1_{a,ord}(K^p, A) →Φ ⊕_{[g] ∈ C_{n−1}(K)} M^0_{a′,ord}(K^p_{n−1,g}, Λ_{n−1})
⊗ A → 0, Φ the sum of the Siegel operators to the boundary components (cusp forms on G_{n−1} =
GU(1,1)) (SU Thm 6.3.10, from Cor. 5.5.9); (iii) for every sufficiently regular algebraic dominant
weight k the canonical surjection h^{S,q}(K^p; Λ_n) ⊗ Λ_n/P_k ↠ h^{S,q}_{k,ord}(K) has nilpotent
kernel, so every ℚ̄_p-valued character of the Λ-adic Hecke algebra at P_k comes from a classical
ordinary eigenform of weight k (SU Thm 6.4.1). Consequently h_{Σ,0}(K′; Λ) is finite over Λ_2 and
its arithmetic points of regular weight are Zariski dense.
Hypothesis: Weights sufficiently regular in the sense of SU 6.2; neat level; p split in K.
Check: The boundary sequence is what lets one subtract an Eisenstein family from a lift and obtain a
cusp form congruent to it.
Source: SU-2014, Thm 6.3.3, p. 91; Thm 6.3.10 and Thm 6.4.1, p. 95; Cor. 5.5.9, p. 78.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L1/eisenstein-datum
A p-adic Eisenstein datum D = (A, 𝕀, 𝐟, ψ, ξ, Σ) consists of the ring of integers A of a finite
extension of ℚ_p containing ℤ[µ_{M_D p}, i, D_K^{1/2}], a normal domain 𝕀 finite over Λ_{W,A}, an
𝕀-adic ordinary eigenform 𝐟 of tame level M, finite idele class characters ψ and ξ of 𝔸_K^×
(ψ|_{𝔸^×} = χ_𝐟), and a finite set of primes Σ containing those dividing M p D_K and the
ramification of ξ. Put Λ_D = 𝕀[[Γ^-_K × Γ_K]], a finite local reduced Λ_2-algebra via α ⊗ β (SU
6.5.1), the characters σ_ψ, σ_ξ, σ_{ξ′} = σ_ξσ^c_ξ, and the semisimple representation ρ_D =
σ_{χ̄_f,0}σ^c_ψε^{-3} ⊕ (ρ_𝐟 ⊗ σ_{χ̄_f,0}σ^{-c}_ξσ^c_ψε^{-2}) ⊕ σ_{χ̄_f,0}ε^{-1}det ρ_𝐟
σ^{-1}_{ξ′}σ^c_ψ of G_K over Frac Λ_D (6.5.2.a). The Eisenstein ideal I_D ⊆ h_D = h_{Σ,0}(K′; Λ_D)
is generated by the coefficients of Q_v(X) − Q_{v,D}(X) (v ∉ Σ), the relations u_t − ∏β_i^{a_i} for
the U_p-operators with the prescribed (β_1,…,β_4), and the central operators Z_{ℓ,0}, Z^{(i)}_{ℓ,0}
minus their values on the Eisenstein characters; 𝔈_D ⊆ Λ_D is the kernel of Λ_D → h_D/I_D, so
Λ_D/𝔈_D ≅ h_D/I_D (6.5.3.a), and 𝕋_D is the localization of h_D at the maximal ideal containing I_D.
The two p-adic L-functions attached to D are L^Σ_{𝐟,K,ξ} ∈ 𝕀[[Γ_K]] (L1/su-padic-l-function) and
L^Σ_{χ̄_𝐟ξ̄′}, the image of the Kubota–Leopoldt function of the Dirichlet character χ̄_𝐟ξ̄′; their
product is L_D.
Hypothesis: ψ exists with ψ|_{𝔸^×} = χ_𝐟 and unramified outside Σ (SU Thm 12.3.1 hypothesis);
(irred)_𝐟 and (dist)_𝐟 when 𝕋_D is used.
Check: The specialization of ρ_D at an arithmetic point with an associated Eisenstein series is the
Galois representation of that Eisenstein series (SU remark after (6.5.2.a)).
Source: SU-2014, §6.5.1–6.5.3, pp. 96–98.
API EisensteinDatum.lambdaD: Λ_D = 𝕀[[Γ^-_K × Γ_K]] with its Λ_2-algebra structure via α ⊗ β.
API EisensteinDatum.rhoD: The semisimple G_K-representation ρ_D of (6.5.2.a), unramified outside Σ,
with det(1 − ρ_D(g)X) ∈ Λ_D[X].
API EisensteinDatum.heckeIdeal: I_D ⊆ h_D generated as in SU 6.5.3.
API EisensteinDatum.ideal: 𝔈_D := ker(Λ_D → h_D/I_D).
API EisensteinDatum.quotientEquiv: Λ_D/𝔈_D ≃ h_D/I_D (6.5.3.a).
API EisensteinDatum.LD: L_D = L^Σ_{𝐟,K,ξ}·L^Σ_{χ̄_𝐟ξ̄′} ∈ Λ_D.
API EisensteinDatum.specialize: At an arithmetic φ ∈ X^a_D with an associated Eisenstein series,
φ(ρ_D) is that series' Galois representation and φ(Q_{v,D}) its Hecke polynomial.
API EisensteinDatum.ext: Transport of A,I, the family and characters preserving their stated
identities transports ρ_D, I_D and 𝔈_D; equality of all generator eigenvalues identifies the ideals.
Test eisensteinDatum_trace_shape: tr ρ_D is the sum of two characters and the trace of a twist of
ρ_𝐟; its reduction modulo the maximal ideal is the residual trace used in Thm 7.3.1, with exactly
one irreducible two-dimensional constituent under (irred)_𝐟.
Test eisensteinDatum_proper: 𝔈_D ≠ Λ_D exactly when some cuspidal ordinary eigensystem is residually
congruent to the eigensystem of ρ_D.
Test eisensteinDatum_rank_count: At an arithmetic quotient I→O of a genuine finite Iwasawa branch,
Λ_D⊗_I O≅O[[Γ_K^-×Γ_K]] has three Iwasawa variables and Krull dimension four. The original I still
has its weight variable; I=O is not a finite extension of O[[W]].
Test eisensteinDatum_not_gl2: Replacing h_D by the GL₂ Hecke algebra of 𝐟 does not give an
Eisenstein ideal of this datum: ρ_D has rank four and its Hecke polynomials Q_v have degree four.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L1/klingen-eisenstein-series
For a cuspidal eigenform f on GU(1,1) (from an elliptic newform and a character ψ), a character ξ of
𝔸_K^× and a choice of local sections at the places of Σ and at p, construct the holomorphic
Klingen-type Eisenstein series E(Z, x; F) on GU(2,2), induced from the Klingen parabolic with Levi
GU(1,1) × Res_{K/ℚ}𝔾_m, of weight κ > 6 (SU §9, 'good Eisenstein series' Prop. 9.3.3), its Hecke
eigenvalues and Satake parameters (SU §9.5–9.6), and its expression through the pullback of a Siegel
Eisenstein series on GU(3,3) along GU(2,2) × GU(1,1) ↪ GU(3,3) paired against f (SU §11, Prop.
11.2.3). The local sections at p and at Σ are chosen as in SU §11.4, so that the Fourier
coefficients c(β, x) are products of local Whittaker integrals with explicit values (SU Cor.
11.7.5–11.7.6, §11.8), the singular ones (det β = 0) being multiples of the L-values L^Σ_K(f, χ̄_fξ,
κ − 1)·L^Σ(χ̄_fξ̄′, κ − 2).
Hypothesis: κ > 6 for the absolutely convergent range; the sections at p have conductor dividing p
as in (12.4.2.a) when the family is formed.
Hypothesis: The doubling/pullback formalism, the Siegel Eisenstein series on unitary groups and the
local doubling integrals are imported; this node owns the Klingen-specific choices and coefficient
formulas.
Check: The constant terms (Siegel operator images) and all singular Fourier coefficients are
divisible by the product of the two L-values; the nonsingular ones are not in general.
Source: SU-2014, §9 (Props 9.2.6, 9.3.3, 9.5.3, 9.6.1, 9.6.2), pp. 113–135.
Source: SU-2014, §11 (Prop. 11.2.3; Props 11.4.13–11.4.23; Props 11.6.1–11.6.2; Cor. 11.6.3; Props
11.7.1–11.7.4; Cors 11.7.5–11.7.6; Prop. 11.8.2), pp. 140–190.
API KlingenEisenstein.series: E(Z, x; F) on GU(2,2) from (f, ψ, ξ, Σ, local sections).
API KlingenEisenstein.pullback: E is the pairing of the pullback of E^{Sieg}_{GU(3,3)} with f
(doubling identity).
API KlingenEisenstein.fourierCoeff: c(β, x) as a product of local Whittaker integrals with the
values of SU Cor. 11.7.5–11.7.6.
API KlingenEisenstein.singular_dvd: For det β = 0, c(β, x) is a multiple of L^Σ_K(f, χ̄_fξ,
κ−1)·L^Σ(χ̄_fξ̄′, κ−2) divided by the period.
API KlingenEisenstein.hecke: E is a Hecke eigenform with Hecke polynomials Q_{v,D} at v ∉ Σ and the
U_p-eigenvalues ∏β_i^{a_i}.
API KlingenEisenstein.siegelOperator: The Siegel-operator image of E at each boundary component is f
(or 0) times the constant-term L-value.
API KlingenEisenstein.ext: Equality follows from equality of all q-expansion coefficients at the
specified cusps; compatible coefficient extension carries the local-section integrals and their
eigenvalues to the extended series.
Test klingen_constant_term_divisible: The Siegel-operator image of E at the standard boundary
component equals the L-value product times f, up to the explicit nonzero archimedean constant of SU
§11.
Test klingen_not_siegel: The Siegel Eisenstein series on GU(2,2) itself (induced from the Siegel
parabolic) has Hecke polynomials of a different shape (four characters, no two-dimensional
constituent) and is not E.
Test klingen_hecke_matches_rhoD: At v ∉ Σ, the Hecke polynomial of E equals φ(Q_{v,D}), the
characteristic polynomial of Frob_v on the specialization of ρ_D (L1/eisenstein-datum).

### AutomorphicCongruences:L1/su-padic-l-function
Let A, 𝕀, 𝐟, ξ and Σ be as in a p-adic Eisenstein datum, and suppose there is a finite A-valued
idele class character ψ with ψ|_{𝔸^×} = χ_𝐟, unramified outside Σ. (i) There is L̃^Σ_{𝐟,K,ξ} ∈
Frac(𝕀) ⊗_𝕀 𝕀_K whose value at every φ ∈ X_{𝕀_K,A}(𝐟, ψ, ξ) is the Petersson-normalized algebraic
part of L^Σ_K(f_φ, χ̄_{f_φ}ξ_φ, κ_φ − 1) (SU Thm 12.3.1(i)); (ii) under (irred)_𝐟 and (dist)_𝐟 there
is L^Σ_{𝐟,K,ξ} ∈ 𝕀_K whose value at φ is u_{f_φ} times the same L-value normalized by the canonical
periods Ω^+_{f_φ}Ω^-_{f_φ}, u_{f_φ} a p-adic unit (SU Thm 12.3.1(ii)). (iii) If moreover χ_𝐟 = 1, ξ
= ξ̄^c with conductor dividing (p), (M, D_K) = 1, M = M^+M^- with M^- squarefree with an odd number
of primes, all inert in K, and ρ̄_𝐟 ramified at every ℓ | M^-, then L^Σ_{𝐟,K,ξ} lies in no prime of
𝕀[[Γ_K]] of the form Q·𝕀[[Γ_K]] with Q a height-one prime of 𝕀[[Γ^+_K]] (SU Prop. 12.3.6, from
Vatsal's µ = 0). Precisely, the common numerator at φ is a(p,f_φ)^{-ord_p Nm(f_{χ̄_f
ξ_φ})}·((κ_φ−2)!)²·g(χ̄_f ξ_φ)·Nm(f_{ξ_φ}d)^{κ_φ−2}·L_K^Σ(f_φ,χ̄_f ξ_φ,κ_φ−1). In (i) divide it by
(−2πi)^{2κ_φ−2}·2^{-3}(2i)^{κ_φ+1}S(f_φ)⟨f_φ,f_φ^c|W_{N_φ}⟩; in (ii) divide by
(−2πi)^{2κ_φ−2}Ω^+_{f_φ}Ω^-_{f_φ} and multiply by u_{f_φ}.
Hypothesis: As stated; the interpolation set X_{𝕀_K,A}(𝐟, ψ, ξ) is defined in SU §12.3 (conductor
conditions at p).
Hypothesis: A contains i, sqrt(D_K), and the local Gauss sums g(ξ_ℓ^c,a_ℓδ_K), g(χ̄_f,ℓ
ξ_ℓ^c,b_ℓδ_K), g(χ_f,ℓ ξ̄′_ℓ) for ℓ∈Σ\{p}, with conductors as in Prop. 12.2.5.
Hypothesis: In (iii), every prime of M⁺ splits in K; the stated inert conditions apply to M⁻.
Check: Integrality (ii) requires (irred) and (dist); without them only (i) holds.
Check: The interpolation uses the canonical periods Ω^±; the anticyclotomic restriction is compared
with Bertolini–Darmon's function only up to a unit (SU 3.6.14).
Source: SU-2014, Thm 12.3.1, p. 199; Prop. 12.3.6, p. 202.
Source: SU-2014, Prop. 12.2.5, author-copy p. 196; Thm 12.3.1, p. 199.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L1/padic-eisenstein-family
Let D be a p-adic Eisenstein datum with cond(ψ_p), cond(ξ_p) | p, (irred)_𝐟 and (dist)_𝐟, and K′ ⊆
K′_D with K′K_p neat. (i) There is ℰ_D ∈ M^{a,ord}(K′; Λ_D) whose specialization at every φ ∈ X″_D
is u_φ·G_{D_φ}/(Ω^+_{f_φ}Ω^-_{f_φ}) with |u_φ|_p = 1, G_{D_φ} the classical Klingen Eisenstein
series (SU Thm 12.4.2(i), Thm 12.4.1); (ii) for x with x_p ∈ Q(ℤ_p) and det β = 0, the β-Fourier
coefficient c_D(β, x) lies in L^Σ_{𝐟,K,ξ}·L^Σ_{χ̄_𝐟ξ̄′}·Λ_D (Thm 12.4.2(ii)); (iii) The full
ordinary Hecke algebra h_D^full acting on all forms has the Eisenstein eigencharacter
λ_D^full:h_D^full→Λ_D. The cuspidal Hecke algebra h_D^cusp only acquires this eigensystem modulo
𝔈_D, through Λ_D/𝔈_D≅h_D^cusp/I_D. SU uses h_D for the full algebra on p. 204 and the cuspidal
algebra in §6.5.3. Consequently, if P ⊂ Λ_D is a height-one prime with ℰ_D ≢ 0 modulo P, then
ord_P(𝔈_D) ≥ ord_P(L_D) (SU Thm 6.5.4): subtracting a lift of ℰ_D's constant terms produces a
cuspidal family congruent to ℰ_D modulo L_D. Here ℰ_D denotes the Eisenstein form and 𝔈_D the
coefficient-ring ideal; a hypothesis that the form is nonzero modulo P concerns ℰ_D.
Hypothesis: (irred)_𝐟, (dist)_𝐟; A satisfies the hypotheses of SU Prop. 12.2.5; conductor condition
(12.4.2.a) at p.
Hypothesis: The conclusion of Thm 6.5.4 is conditional on ℰ_D being nonzero modulo P;
L1/nonsingular-coefficient-coprimality supplies this.
Check: The congruence is with the whole family ℰ_D, not with an individual Eisenstein series; an
interpolation formula for ℰ_D alone does not give it.
Check: Excluded case: if ℰ_D ≡ 0 mod P no bound is claimed.
Source: SU-2014, Thms 12.4.1–12.4.2, pp. 202–203; Thm 6.5.4 and the paragraph before it, p. 98.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L1/nonsingular-coefficient-coprimality
Let A be the ring of integers of a finite extension of ℚ_p, 𝕀 a domain finite over Λ_{W,A}, 𝐟 ∈
M^{ord}(M, 1; 𝕀) an 𝕀-adic newform with (irred)_𝐟 and (dist)_𝐟, and Σ_0 ⊇ {ℓ | pMD_K}. There is a
finite Σ ⊇ Σ_0 such that for D = (A, 𝕀, 𝐟, 1, 1, Σ) the set C_D of nonsingular coefficients c_D(β_i,
x) of ℰ_D satisfies: (i) every height-one prime of Λ_D containing C_D is of the form PΛ_D with P a
height-one prime of 𝕀[[Γ^+_K]]; (ii) if M = M^+M^- with M^- squarefree with an odd number of prime
factors, all inert in K, and ρ̄_𝐟 ramified at every ℓ | M^-, then no height-one prime of Λ_D
contains both L^Σ_{𝐟,K,1} and C_D (SU Prop. 13.4.1).
Hypothesis: The hypotheses of SU Prop. 12.2.5 on A; the auxiliary primes of Prop. 13.2.5 (a set of
primes split in K not dividing the level) are part of the choice of Σ.
Check: This is what makes ℰ_D nonzero modulo every height-one prime dividing L^Σ_{𝐟,K}, so that Thm
6.5.4 applies at those primes.
Source: SU-2014, Prop. 13.4.1 and the paragraph before it, p. 217; Props 13.2.5, 13.3.4, pp.
210–213.
Source: SU-2014, §1.4.5, p. 9.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L1/gu22-galois-pseudocharacter
For a neat K = K^0_pK^p ⊂ G(𝔸_f) and a finite set S of primes containing p and the primes where K_ℓ
is not hyperspecial, there is a pseudo-representation T^S_{K^p}: G_K → h_{S,0}(K^p) of dimension
four such that tr R_p(π) = λ^{ord}_π ∘ T^S_{K^p} for every p-ordinary cuspidal π of weight k with
π_f^{K^p} ≠ 0 (SU Prop. 7.2.1), where R_p(π) is the four-dimensional Galois representation attached
to π (imported from AutomorphicGaloisRepresentationsPartII: polarized, unramified outside S,
crystalline at p when π_p is unramified, with the ordinary filtration at the primes above p). For a
datum D, T_D is its composite with h_D, R_D the semisimple representation over the total fraction
ring of h_D with trace T_D, and for each irreducible component J, R_J is either absolutely
irreducible or, over a finite extension of Frac J, R_1 ⊕ R_2 with two-dimensional R_i satisfying
R_i^c ≅ R_i^∨ ⊗ ε^{-3}ω^{1+c}_{K′} (SU Thm 7.3.1).
Hypothesis: k regular for the attachment of R_p(π) (SU Thm 7.1.1, from Morel and Shin as explained
in Skinner 2010); (irred)_𝐟 and (dist)_𝐟 for Thm 7.3.1.
Hypothesis: The Galois representations of cuspidal representations of GU(2,2) and their local–global
compatibility at p are imported (RT-AREA-iwasawa-1/6), not constructed here.
Check: The residual pseudo-character of 𝕋_D is tr ρ̄_D: two characters and one irreducible
two-dimensional constituent.
Source: SU-2014, Thm 7.1.1, p. 99; Prop. 7.2.1 and its proof, p. 101.
Source: SU-2014, Thm 7.3.1 and its proof, pp. 103–105.
API GU22PseudoChar.T: T^S_{K^p}: G_K → h_{S,0}(K^p), a continuous four-dimensional
pseudo-representation.
API GU22PseudoChar.specialize: λ^{ord}_π ∘ T = tr R_p(π) for every ordinary cuspidal π of level K^p.
API GU22PseudoChar.frob: T(frob_v) = T_v for v ∉ S ∪ {p}; the characteristic polynomial of frob_v is
the Hecke polynomial Q_v.
API GU22PseudoChar.polarization: T(c g c) = T(g^{-1})·(similitude character) — the polarization
inherited from each R_p(π).
API GU22PseudoChar.components: Dichotomy of Thm 7.3.1 for components J of 𝕋_D.
API GU22PseudoChar.residual: T_D modulo the maximal ideal is tr ρ̄_D.
API GU22PseudoChar.ext: Continuous pseudocharacters agreeing at all unramified Frobenius classes
agree by Chebotarev density; compatible Hecke quotient maps transport the pseudocharacter.
Test gu22PseudoChar_dimension: T(1) = 4.
Test gu22PseudoChar_eisenstein_point: The cuspidal pseudocharacter modulo I_D agrees with tr ρ_D
modulo 𝔈_D through Λ_D/𝔈_D≅h_D^cusp/I_D. It need not admit an Eisenstein eigencharacter into Λ_D
itself.
Test gu22PseudoChar_not_gl2: A pseudo-character of dimension two (the GL₂ one of 𝐟) does not satisfy
GU22PseudoChar.specialize: its value at 1 is 2.
Test gu22PseudoChar_cap_excluded: No component J has R_J = character ⊕ character ⊕ irreducible
two-dimensional (the CAP shape), under (irred)_𝐟 and (dist)_𝐟.

