### AutomorphicCongruences:L2s/semi-ordinary-weight-space
Let K be imaginary quadratic with p = v_0v̄_0 split, and GU(3,1) the unitary similitude group of
signature (3,1), so that GU(3,1)(ℚ_p) ≅ GL_4(ℚ_p) × ℚ_p^× and the diagonal torus of the p-adic group
gives a four-coordinate p-adic weight space (after fixing the similitude character) of characters
(a_1, a_2, a_3; b_1). The semi-ordinary torus T_so(ℤ_p) ⊂ T(ℤ_p) is the two-dimensional subtorus of
weights (0, 0, t^+; t^-), with Iwasawa algebra Λ_so = ℤ_p[[T_so(1+pℤ_p)]]. On the p-adic forms V on
the Igusa tower put U_p := U^+_{p,2}U^+_{p,3}U^-_{p,1}; for every f ∈ V the limit e_so f :=
lim_{r→∞} U_p^{r!} f exists and defines the semi-ordinary projector e_so. A classical form is
semi-ordinary if it is fixed by e_so; this asks unit U_p-eigenvalues only in the two directions of
T_so, so it allows forms whose GL_2 part at p is not ordinary (a_p = 0 is allowed). Keep the finite
character of T_so(ℤ_p)/T_so(1+pℤ_p) when using the full group algebra; it is not another
power-series variable.
Hypothesis: p ≥ 3 split in K; the operators U^±_{p,i} are those of CLW (3.0.7)–(3.0.8) on p-integral
forms.
Hypothesis: The convergence of U_p^{r!} on every f uses the finiteness property (F) of CLW Thm
4.6.1, proved there; the generic profinite projector is imported.
Check: Semi-ordinary is weaker than ordinary: an ordinary form is semi-ordinary, while a form whose
GL_2 constituent at p has a_p = 0 can be semi-ordinary and is killed by the ordinary projector.
Source: CLW-v1, Thm 2.9.1(1), p. 13; Thm 4.6.1 and proof, p. 29.
Source: CLW-v1, §1, p. 3.
API SemiOrd.torus: The two-dimensional torus T_so; Λ_so=Z_p[[T_so(1+pZ_p)]] and its finite-character
components inside Z_p[[T_so(Z_p)]].
API SemiOrd.Up: U_p = U^+_{p,2}U^+_{p,3}U^-_{p,1} acting on p-adic forms.
API SemiOrd.proj: e_so = lim U_p^{r!}, an idempotent commuting with the Hecke operators away from p.
API SemiOrd.proj_idem: e_so ∘ e_so = e_so.
API SemiOrd.ord_le_so: e_so ∘ e_ord = e_ord: ordinary forms are semi-ordinary.
API SemiOrd.weight: The weight map from Λ_so-families to characters (τ^+, τ^-) of T_so(ℤ_p).
API SemiOrd.ext: Two semi-ordinary forms are equal iff their ambient forms are equal; membership is
e_so(f)=f. The projector transports along equivariant maps.
Test semiOrd_projector_eigenvalue: On a classical eigenform whose U_p-eigenvalue is a p-adic unit u,
U_p^{r!}f = u^{r!}f → f because u^{r!} → 1 in ℤ_p^× along r!; on an eigenform with U_p-eigenvalue in
pℤ_p the limit is 0.
Test semiOrd_nonordinary_survives: In the unitary normalization, a good supersingular weight-two GL₂
constituent has Satake valuations 0,0. It can occur in CLW’s semi-ordinary Klingen family although
its classical GL₂ U_p roots have valuation 1/2,1/2. The extreme unitary valuations ±1/2 describe the
ordinary case; they are not the supersingular example.
Test semiOrd_two_dimensional: After fixing a_1=a_2=0, Λ_so has two weight variables and Krull
dimension three over Z_p; the finite torus character is retained separately. The unfixed weight
tuple has four coordinates.
Test semiOrd_not_ordinary_projector: Using the ordinary projector in place of e_so on the weight-two
forms of a supersingular π gives zero, so it cannot produce the Klingen family of
L2s/semi-ordinary-klingen-family.

### AutomorphicCongruences:L2s/semi-ordinary-hida-theory
